<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Sacred · c. 2600 BCE · Ancient Near East</p><h1 class="sg-title">Kesh Temple Hymn</h1><p class="sg-kid" data-say>One of the oldest songs ever written down: a Sumerian hymn from about 2600 BCE praising the temple of Kesh and its goddess.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>anonymous (the hymn names the goddess Nisaba as the one who wrote it down)</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>Sumerians</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>Sumerian</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>Kesh, Sumer (present-day Iraq); copies from Abu Salabikh, Nippur, Isin, Kish, Ur and Susa</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>c. 2600 BCE (the archaic copy from Abu Salabikh); later copies of the Old Babylonian period</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Sacred</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (an ancient text; the Sumerian lines printed here and their translation are those of the Electronic Text Corpus of Sumerian Literature (Oxford), licensed CC BY-NC-SA 3.0)</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>The Kesh temple hymn, also called the Liturgy to Nintud, is a Sumerian hymn written on clay tablets as early as 2600 BCE; with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/instructions-of-shuruppak">Instructions of Shuruppak</a> it is the oldest surviving literature in the world. It praises the temple of Kesh, a city whose chief goddess was Nintud. In its 134 lines, formally divided into eight songs or &quot;houses&quot;, the god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/enlil">Enlil</a> comes forth, looks over all the lands and speaks the praises of Kesh, and the goddess Nisaba, patron of writing, sets them down on tablets; such a framing makes the text the work of gods, handed down to humans, and so gives it its authority. Each of the eight songs ends with the same three wondering questions about the greatness of Kesh, of its hero Acgi and of its lady Nintud.</p><p>Fragments were found among the tablets that the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pennsylvania">Pennsylvania</a> Museum excavated from the temple library at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nippur">Nippur</a> in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>. Hugo Radau first published one of them in 1909, and Stephen Herbert Langdon translated it in 1915; in 1913 Langdon had already published a translation from a four-sided perforated prism from Nippur in the Ashmolean Museum in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxford">Oxford</a>, with some 145 lines in eight sections. He called it &quot;A Liturgy to Nintud, Goddess of Creation&quot; and read its refrain as speaking of the creation of man and woman, the biblical Adam and Eve. George Aaron Barton, translating another tablet in 1918, rejected this: he found no allusion to the creation of man and thought the text celebrated the early state, perhaps the founding, of a town. Edward Chiera, Hermann Volrath Hilprecht, Samuel Noah Kramer and others published further copies, from Nippur and from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/istanbul">Istanbul</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> and Baltimore collections, and more came from excavations at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isin">Isin</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kish">Kish</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/susa">Susa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ur">Ur</a>. Miguel Civil produced a composite text in 1992; Gene Gragg, who translated it in 1969, called it one of the best preserved literary texts of the Old Babylonian period.</p><p>Robert D. Biggs translated an exceptionally archaic version from Tell <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abu-salabikh">Abu Salabikh</a>, which he dated to about 2600 BCE by its likeness to the tablets of Shuruppak; radiocarbon dating of the site later gave 2550–2520 BCE, slightly later than he proposed. Though the Abu Salabikh copies are some eight centuries older than the copies known before, they differ surprisingly little from them except in spelling, so that, as Biggs wrote, the Old Babylonian version is a faithful copy of a text that had already been fixed in the Sumerian literary tradition for centuries. He suggested that Abu Salabikh might be Kesh itself, while noting that it is not near Adab, as Kesh was described, and that &quot;Kesh&quot; might be a variant spelling of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kish">Kish</a>.</p><p>The hymn moves from Nisaba's record of the choosing and praise of Kesh to the temple's cosmic size, likened to the moon in the sky, reaching up to heaven and down to the underworld, and then to its herds of oxen and sheep, the trees whose wood built it, its gods and functions, and its gate, courtyard, doors and walls at its dedication. Jeremy Black suggests it describes the statues of winged lions and wild bulls set at temple entrances, and it tells of music at the temple's ceremonies, of drums and the growl of the bull's horn; Samuel Noah Kramer proposed that its musical terms name hymns sung with the lyre, with a kind of drum and with another string instrument. It ends with an admonition repeated four times, both a warning and an invitation to the divine presence: to come near the city and its temple, and yet not to come near. Such ambivalence about approaching temples has crucially influenced the development of Jewish and Christian mysticism.</p><p>Langdon took Nintud, the chief goddess of Kesh, for a form of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ninlil">Ninlil</a>: the Ninlil of Kesh, where her character as goddess of begetting was emphasised, the goddess bearing different names in different places. By the time of the later Babylonian copies Kesh was no longer a major settlement, though Black presumed that the temple of Nintud still worked; Wilfred G. Lambert noted that the sanctuary of Kesh was the centre of the goddess <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ninhursag">Ninhursag</a>'s cult from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/early-dynastic-period">Early Dynastic period</a> to the Old Babylonian dynasty, after which it lost its importance. Raymond de Hoop has pointed to close parallels in wording and imagery between the Sumerian temple hymns and the sayings about Joseph and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judah">Judah</a> in Genesis 49.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A hymn in praise of a temple: the house of the goddess Nintud at Kesh, chosen by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/enlil">Enlil</a> and recorded by Nisaba, is praised as a mountain reaching heaven, a crown and a rainbow, founded on the waters below the earth, rich in herds and served by gods and heroes. Its eight songs each close by asking whether anything as great as Kesh, its hero and its lady could ever be born again, and the last warns the worshipper to approach the temple with awe. The mood is solemn, grand and reverent.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-blocks sg-blocks-2"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-1"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">Sumerian</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sux">nam₂-nun-e nam₂-nun-e e₂-ta nam-ta-ab-ed₂</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sux">{d}en-lil₂ nam₂-nun-e e₂-ta nam-ta-ab-ed₂</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sux">nam₂-nun-e nam-lugal-la e₂-ta nam-ta-ab-ed₂</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sux">{d}en-lil₂ kur-kur-ra igi mi-ni-in-il₂-il₂-i</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sux">{d}en-lil₂-ra kur ni₂-ba mu-na-il₂-il₂-i</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sux">an ub-da 4 {d}en-lil₂-ra {ĝiš}kiri₆-gin₇ mu-na-sig₇</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sux">keš{ki} saĝ il₂ mu-na-ni-in-ĝal₂</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sux">keš{ki} kur-kur-ra saĝ il₂-bi</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sux">{d}en-lil₂-le keš{ki} za₃-mi₂ am₃-ma-ab-be₂</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sux">{d}nisaba nu-ka-aš-bi-im</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sux">inim-bi-ta sa-gin₇ im-da-an-sur</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sux">dub-ba sar-sar šu-še₃ al-ĝa₂-ĝa₂</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sux">e₂ muš₃ kalam-ma gud ḫuš aratta</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sux">e₂ keš{ki} muš₃ kalam-ma gud ḫuš aratta</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sux">ḫur-saĝ-da mu₂-a an-da gu₂ la₂-a</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sux">e₂-kur-da mu₂-a kur-ra saĝ il₂-bi</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sux">abzu-gin₇ ri-a ḫur-saĝ-gin₇ sig₇-sig₇-ga</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sux">keš{ki}-gin₇ rib-ba lu₂ ši-in-ga-tum₂-mu</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sux">ur-saĝ-bi {d}{aš}aš₇-gi₄-gin₇ rib-ba ama ši-in-ga-u₃-tud</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sux">nin-bi {d}nin-tud-gin₇ rib-ba-ra a-ba-a igi mu-ni-in-du₈</span></p></div></div><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-1"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-e">In English</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>The princely one, the princely one came forth from the house.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Enlil, the princely one, came forth from the house.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>The princely one came forth royally from the house.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Enlil lifted his glance over all the lands, and the lands raised themselves to Enlil.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>The four corners of heaven became green for Enlil like a garden.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Keš was positioned there for him with head uplifted, and as Keš lifted its head among all the lands, Enlil spoke the praises of Keš.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Nisaba was its decision-maker (?);</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>with its words she wove it intricately like a net.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Written on tablets it was held in her hands: House, platform of the Land, important fierce bull! House Keš, platform of the Land, important fierce bull! Growing as high as the hills, embracing the heavens, growing as high as E-kur, lifting its head among the mountains! Rooted in the abzu, verdant like the mountains! Will anyone else bring forth something as great as Keš? Will any other mother ever give birth to someone as great as its hero Ašgi? Who has ever seen anyone as great as its lady Nintud?</span></p></div></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Original: J.A. Black, G. Cunningham, E. Fluckiger-Hawker, E. Robson and G. Zólyomi, The Electronic Text Corpus of Sumerian Literature (Oxford, 1998-2006), 4.80.2, lines 1-20. Text from <a href="https://etcsl.orinst.ox.ac.uk/section4/c4802.htm" target="_blank" rel="noopener">the Electronic Text Corpus of Sumerian Literature (ETCSL)</a>; licensed CC BY-NC-SA 3.0, for non-commercial use with credit.</p><p>English: J.A. Black, G. Cunningham, E. Fluckiger-Hawker, E. Robson and G. Zólyomi, The Electronic Text Corpus of Sumerian Literature (Oxford, 1998-2006), 4.80.2, translation of lines 1-20. Text from <a href="https://etcsl.orinst.ox.ac.uk/section4/tr4802.htm" target="_blank" rel="noopener">the Electronic Text Corpus of Sumerian Literature (ETCSL)</a>; licensed CC BY-NC-SA 3.0, for non-commercial use with credit.</p></div></section>
</div>
